/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Example_GeneratorsLUTEnvelope LUT envelope
 * @ingroup DspCore_Examples_Generators
 * @brief Use a normalized table as an envelope shape over an output range.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_GeneratorsLUTEnvelope_Goal Goal
 * Map a five-element normalized curve to output samples, then show that a
 * singleton shape remains a valid constant envelope.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_GeneratorsLUTEnvelope_Run Run
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./build/macos_arm64/bin/Debug/DspCore_Generators_LUTEnvelope_d
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_GeneratorsLUTEnvelope_Output Output
 * @code{.text}
 * LUT envelope[0]=0/1000; expected=0/1000
 * LUT envelope[1]=125/1000; expected=125/1000
 * LUT envelope[2]=250/1000; expected=250/1000
 * LUT envelope[3]=625/1000; expected=625/1000
 * LUT envelope[4]=1000/1000; expected=1000/1000
 * Singleton shape=0.25 maps [2,10] to 4 in all five output samples
 * Overall: PASS
 * @endcode
 *
 * A one-point LUT has no interpolation span, yet still has a well-defined
 * constant result; this is useful for parameterized envelope pipelines.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_GeneratorsLUTEnvelope_Code Implementation
 * \include Generators/LUTEnvelope/Shared.cpp
 */
